1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,520
About 1 in 20 Americans deal with hyperhidrosis, and hands and feet are two of the most common spots — if yours are damp no matter the season, a few daily habits are usually keeping it going.

2
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:23,520
Sweat glands in your palms and soles respond more to stress and nerve signals than to actual heat, so an overactive sympathetic nervous system can trigger dripping even in a cold room.

3
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:27,120
The sock and shoe combo is one of the biggest factors.

4
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:37,760
Cotton absorbs moisture but doesn't pull it away from your skin, so once it's damp it stays damp — and sealed, rubber-soled shoes trap that moisture with nowhere to go.

5
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:41,520
Wearing the same pair of shoes two days in a row makes it worse.

6
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:51,760
Shoes need 24 to 48 hours to fully dry out between wears, and skipping that means you're stepping into damp material before your feet even start sweating.

7
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:53,680
Caffeine plays a role too.

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:03,440
It stimulates the nervous system that controls sweat glands, so heavy coffee or energy drink intake can trigger more sweating in your hands and feet specifically.

9
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:14,480
Switch to moisture-wicking socks made from merino wool or synthetic blends instead of cotton, and rotate between at least two pairs of shoes so each one gets a full day to dry.

10
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:23,760
An aluminum chloride antiperspirant applied to clean, dry feet at night can cut sweating noticeably, and cutting back on caffeine helps too.

11
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:32,160
If sweating is heavy enough to interfere with daily life, a dermatologist can walk you through iontophoresis or other clinical options.
